INT. RIVERBOAT STATEROOM - NIGHT

1871. A cramped cabin lit by a swaying oil lamp. A trunk, a washstand, a narrow bunk. The paddlewheel thuds somewhere beneath the floor. Rain streaks the porthole.

CAPTAIN SILAS BARROW, 50s, in a rumpled blue coat, sits on the trunk with a ledger on his knee. Across from him, in the only chair, sits MADAME CELESTINE ROUX, 40s, black lace, a small valise in her lap. She has not taken her gloves off.

SILAS
You've paid for passage to Natchez, Madame Roux.

CELESTINE
I have.

SILAS
We reach Natchez at dawn.

CELESTINE
I am aware.

SILAS
Then I wonder why you ask to speak with me at midnight.

CELESTINE
(to herself)
Because midnight is when truths wake.

SILAS
I beg your pardon?

CELESTINE
Nothing, Captain. A habit of mine.

He closes the ledger. The lamp swings. Shadows run across the walls and back.

SILAS
Madame, I run a respectable boat.

CELESTINE
No one doubts it.

SILAS
Then you'll understand that when a lady boards with no luggage but a valise, and a name that is not the one on her papers, I am obliged to ask.

CELESTINE
Ask.

SILAS
Who are you?

CELESTINE
I am the sister of the man you hanged in Vicksburg.

Silas stands. The ledger slides to the floor. Celestine does not move. The paddlewheel thuds on.

SILAS
(simultaneously)
I did not hang him.

^CELESTINE
(simultaneously)
You signed the paper.

They stare. Silas lowers himself back onto the trunk.

SILAS
That was war, Madame.

CELESTINE
War ended in sixty-five.

SILAS
He was a spy.

CELESTINE
He was a boy of nineteen with a letter.

SILAS
Madame Roux--

CELESTINE
You may call me Marguerite. That is my name.

SILAS
(slowly)
Marguerite.

She opens the valise. Inside, on a bed of black velvet, lie a revolver and a folded paper. Silas looks at them for a long moment.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(muttering)
So it ends.

MARGUERITE
No. That is for you to choose.

She takes out the paper, unfolds it, and lays it on his knee. He reads it. His lips move. He finishes. He looks up.

SILAS
This is a confession.

MARGUERITE
It is yours to sign.

SILAS
And if I do not?

She lifts the revolver from the velvet and sets it beside the paper on his knee, butt first.

MARGUERITE
Then you will have both.

Rain hammers the porthole. The lamp steadies. Silas reaches slowly for the pen on the washstand.
